<body data-focus="both">

<div class="hero">
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?
    <span class="zh-title" lang="zh-CN">天空为什么是蓝色的？</span>
  </h1>
  <p>Sunlight is scattered by the air — short (blue) wavelengths scatter far more than long (red) ones.
     <span lang="zh-CN">阳光被空气散射——短波（蓝）比长波（红）散射得多得多。</span></p>
</div>

<nav>
  <button data-target="en-toddler">🍼 3-Year-Old · 3岁</button>
  <button data-target="en-teen">🎓 High School · 高中生</button>
  <button data-target="en-phd">🔬 PhD · 博士</button>
  <span class="spacer"></span>
  <span class="hint">Click a card to flash its counterpart · 点击卡片高亮对应译文</span>
  <button id="focusBtn" title="Cycle focus mode">Focus: Both · 双语</button>
</nav>

<main>

  <!-- ============ TODDLER ============ -->
  <article class="card card-en" id="en-toddler" lang="en">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag en">EN</span><h2>🍼 For a 3-Year-Old</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's really all the colors of a rainbow mixed together.</p>
      <p>The air is full of super-tiny, super-tiny specks — so small you can't see them, even with glasses.</p>
      <p>When the sunlight hits those tiny specks, the <strong>blue</strong> colors bounce around the most.</p>
      <p>The blue bounces all over the sky, so when you look up, you see blue!</p>
      <p>That's why the sky is blue. 🌈</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card card-zh" id="zh-toddler" lang="zh-CN">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag zh">中文</span><h2>🍼 给3岁小朋友</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但它其实是彩虹的所有颜色混在一起的。</p>
      <p>空气里充满了超级超级小的微粒——小到就算戴上眼镜也看不见。</p>
      <p>当阳光照到这些微小微粒上时，<strong>蓝色</strong>的光反弹得最多。</p>
      <p>蓝色光在整片天空到处反弹，所以你抬头就能看到蓝色！</p>
      <p>这就是天空是蓝色的原因。🌈</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- ============ HIGH SCHOOL ============ -->
  <article class="card card-en" id="en-teen" lang="en">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag en">EN</span><h2>🎓 For a High School Teenager</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p>Sunlight is "white," but it's actually a mixture of all visible wavelengths (~400–700 nm). When it enters the atmosphere, it encounters nitrogen (N₂) and oxygen (O₂) molecules. These molecules are <em>much</em> smaller than the wavelength of visible light (a molecule is ~10⁻¹⁰ m; light is ~10⁻⁷ m).</p>
      <p>When an electromagnetic wave hits such a small molecule, its electric field makes the molecule's electrons oscillate. Those oscillating charges radiate — i.e., the molecule <strong>scatters</strong> the light in all directions. This is called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</p>
      <p>The key physics: the strength of Rayleigh scattering scales as <strong>1/λ⁴</strong>. That means shorter wavelengths are scattered dramatically more:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Blue light (~450 nm) is scattered about <strong>4–5× more strongly</strong> than red light (~650 nm), since (650/450)⁴ ≈ 4.3.</li>
        <li>Violet (~400 nm) is scattered even more than blue.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So why isn't the sky violet? Three reasons:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The Sun emits less violet than blue (its ~5,800 K spectrum peaks in the visible and falls off toward the violet).</li>
        <li>Your eyes are less sensitive to violet than to blue.</li>
        <li>Some violet light is absorbed high in the atmosphere by ozone.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Putting it together:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Looking up:</strong> you see blue light that has been scattered toward you from all over the sky → blue sky.</li>
        <li><strong>Looking at the Sun:</strong> you see the direct sunlight, which has lost some of its blue → white/yellowish.</li>
        <li><strong>At sunset:</strong> sunlight travels through a much longer path of atmosphere. The blue gets scattered out of your line of sight almost completely, and the surviving direct light is reddish → red sunsets.</li>
        <li><strong>Clouds:</strong> water droplets are <em>much</em> larger than the wavelength of light, so they scatter all colors equally (this is <strong>Mie scattering</strong>) → clouds look white, not blue.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bonus"><strong>Fun bonus:</strong> on Mars, the sky is butterscotch-colored because its dusty atmosphere scatters in a different way — and the sunsets there are actually blue.</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card card-zh" id="zh-teen" lang="zh-CN">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag zh">中文</span><h2>🎓 给高中生</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p>阳光是"白色"的，但实际上它是所有可见光波长（约400–700纳米）的混合。当它进入大气层时，会遇到氮气（N₂）和氧气（O₂）分子。这些分子<em>远</em>小于可见光的波长（分子约10⁻¹⁰米，光约10⁻⁷米）。</p>
      <p>当电磁波打到这么小的分子上时，它的电场会让分子中的电子振荡。这些振荡的电荷会向外辐射——也就是说，分子会把光向四面八方<strong>散射</strong>。这就是所谓的<strong>瑞利散射</strong>。</p>
      <p>关键物理规律：瑞利散射的强度按 <strong>1/λ⁴</strong> 变化。也就是说，波长越短，被散射得越强烈：</p>
      <ul>
        <li>蓝光（约450纳米）比红光（约650纳米）被散射强约 <strong>4–5倍</strong>，因为 (650/450)⁴ ≈ 4.3。</li>
        <li>紫光（约400纳米）比蓝光被散射得更强。</li>
      </ul>
      <p>那为什么天空不是紫色的呢？原因有三：</p>
      <ol>
        <li>太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少（其约5,800开尔文的光谱在可见光区达到峰值，向紫色方向衰减）。</li>
        <li>你的眼睛对紫光的敏感度低于蓝光。</li>
        <li>一部分紫光在高层大气中被臭氧吸收。</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>综合起来：</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>抬头看：</strong>你看到的是从天空各处散射到你眼中的蓝光 → 蓝天。</li>
        <li><strong>看太阳：</strong>你看到的是直射阳光，它已经损失了一部分蓝光 → 白色/偏黄。</li>
        <li><strong>日落时：</strong>阳光穿过的大气路径长得多。蓝光几乎被完全散射出你的视线，剩下的直射光偏红 → 红色日落。</li>
        <li><strong>云：</strong>水滴比光的波长大得多，因此它们对所有颜色的散射都差不多（这就是<strong>米氏散射</strong>）→ 云看起来是白色的，而不是蓝色的。</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bonus"><strong>趣味补充：</strong>在火星上，天空呈焦糖色，因为其多尘的大气散射方式不同——而那里的日落实际上是蓝色的。</div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- ============ PHYSICS PHD ============ -->
  <article class="card card-en" id="en-phd" lang="en">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag en">EN</span><h2>🔬 For a Physics PhD</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <h3>Microscopic mechanism</h3>
      <p>For a molecule with isotropic polarizability α (SI units, C·m²/V), an incident plane wave induces a dipole p = αE₀. In the far field (dipole approximation, valid for size parameter x = 2πr/λ ≪ 1 — here x ~ 10⁻³ for air molecules), the scattered field amplitude scales as ω²p ∝ k²αE₀, so the scattered <em>intensity</em> scales as k⁴α². This is the origin of the 1/λ⁴ law.</p>

      <h3>Cross sections</h3>
      <p>The differential cross section (averaged over molecular orientations, neglecting the small anisotropy) is</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">dσ</span><span class="den">dΩ</span></span>
        <span class="op">=</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">k⁴α²</span><span class="den">32πε₀²</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span><span>(1 + cos²θ)</span>
      </div>
      <p>which integrates to the total Rayleigh cross section</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span>σ</span>
        <span class="op">=</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">8π³</span><span class="den">3</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">α²</span><span class="den">ε₀²λ⁴</span></span>
        <span class="op">≈</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">8π³</span><span class="den">3</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">(n² − 1)²</span><span class="den">N²λ⁴</span></span>
      </div>
      <p>using the dilute-gas relation α = ε₀(n²−1)/N from Lorentz–Lorenz. For N₂ at 550 nm, α ≈ 1.74×10⁻⁴⁰ C·m²/V gives σ ≈ 4.5–5×10⁻³¹ m².</p>

      <h3>Optical depth: single scattering is a good approximation</h3>
      <p>With a molecular column of ~2×10²⁹ m⁻² (scale height ~8 km), the vertical Rayleigh optical depth is τ(550 nm) ≈ 0.1 and τ(450 nm) ≈ 0.22. Since τ ≪ 1, the blue sky is predominantly a <strong>single-scattering</strong> phenomenon; multiple scattering adds a small whitish depolarizing component.</p>

      <h3>Why blue, not violet?</h3>
      <p>The observed sky spectrum is a convolution of (i) the solar spectrum (5778 K blackbody, falling toward the violet), (ii) the 1/λ⁴ scattering factor, (iii) the eye's photopic sensitivity (peaking near 555 nm, falling in the violet), and (iv) ozone absorption (Huggins/Chappuis bands) removing part of the 300–350 nm. The result peaks around 450–480 nm — "sky blue," i.e., a broad mixture, not a monochromatic color.</p>

      <h3>Angular and polarization structure</h3>
      <p>The (1+cos²θ) phase function means forward and backward scattering are strongest. Rayleigh scattering polarizes the scattered field in the plane perpendicular to the scattering plane; the degree of polarization is maximal (in the pure limit, 100%) at θ = 90° and zero along the solar and anti-solar directions. Observed daytime polarization reaches ~70–80%, reduced by multiple scattering and aerosols. The sky is bluest and most saturated near the anti-solar point.</p>

      <h3>Why sunsets are red</h3>
      <p>At low solar elevation, the slant air mass grows as ~sec z (diverging toward the horizon, air mass ≳ 30–40). The direct beam suffers exp(−τ(λ)) attenuation with τ(450 nm)/τ(700 nm) ≈ 5.8, so the direct solar spectrum is strongly red-shifted while the scattered skylight overhead remains blue — the classic red-sun, blue-dome contrast. Aerosols (Mie, with a much flatter wavelength dependence) further deepen the red.</p>

      <h3>What the Rayleigh picture doesn't cover</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mie scattering</strong> from aerosols (r ≳ λ): forward-peaked, weakly wavelength-dependent; dominates haze and the white appearance of polluted skies.</li>
        <li><strong>Clouds:</strong> droplets with r ≫ λ → geometric-optics/Mie regime, all wavelengths scattered nearly equally → white/gray.</li>
        <li><strong>Inelastic channels:</strong> rotational/vibrational Raman scattering exists but the elastic (Rayleigh) line is overwhelmingly dominant.</li>
        <li><strong>Exoplanetary analogues:</strong> Rayleigh scattering explains the blue of Neptune/Uranus (with CH₄ removing the red), while Titan's orange sky is a photochemical haze (aerosol Mie), not molecular Rayleigh scattering.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Historical note</h3>
      <p>Lord Rayleigh (1871) derived the 1/λ⁴ law for a continuous dielectric sphere; J. J. Thomson independently obtained the dipole-scattering result. The quantum (photon) description reproduces it as elastic scattering off a bound electron system, with the dipole matrix elements giving the same frequency dependence.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card card-zh" id="zh-phd" lang="zh-CN">
    <div class="card-head"><span class="tag zh">中文</span><h2>🔬 给物理学博士</h2></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <h3>微观机制</h3>
      <p>对于各向同性极化率为 α 的分子（SI单位，C·m²/V），入射平面波会感应出电偶极矩 p = αE₀。在远场区（偶极近似，适用于尺寸参数 x = 2πr/λ ≪ 1 的情形——对空气分子，x ~ 10⁻³），散射场振幅按 ω²p ∝ k²αE₀ 变化，因此散射<em>强度</em>按 k⁴α² 变化。这就是 1/λ⁴ 规律的由来。</p>

      <h3>截面</h3>
      <p>微分截面（对分子取向平均，忽略小的各向异性）为</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">dσ</span><span class="den">dΩ</span></span>
        <span class="op">=</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">k⁴α²</span><span class="den">32πε₀²</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span><span>(1 + cos²θ)</span>
      </div>
      <p>积分后得到瑞利总截面</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span>σ</span>
        <span class="op">=</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">8π³</span><span class="den">3</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">α²</span><span class="den">ε₀²λ⁴</span></span>
        <span class="op">≈</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">8π³</span><span class="den">3</span></span>
        <span class="op">·</span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">(n² − 1)²</span><span class="den">N²λ⁴</span></span>
      </div>
      <p>其中使用了洛伦兹–洛伦茨（Lorentz–Lorenz）稀薄气体关系 α = ε₀(n²−1)/N。对550纳米处的N₂，α ≈ 1.74×10⁻⁴⁰ C·m²/V，可得 σ ≈ 4.5–5×10⁻³¹ m²。</p>

      <h3>光学厚度：单次散射是很好的近似</h3>
      <p>分子柱密度约2×10²⁹ m⁻²（标高约8千米），垂直方向的瑞利光学厚度为 τ(550 nm) ≈ 0.1，τ(450 nm) ≈ 0.22。由于 τ ≪ 1，蓝天主要是<strong>单次散射</strong>现象；多次散射只贡献少量去极化的白色成分。</p>

      <h3>为什么是蓝色而不是紫色？</h3>
      <p>观测到的天空光谱是以下因素的卷积：(i) 太阳光谱（5778 K 黑体，向紫色方向衰减）；(ii) 1/λ⁴ 散射因子；(iii) 人眼明视觉灵敏度（峰值在约555纳米，在紫光区下降）；(iv) 臭氧吸收（Huggins/Chappuis 吸收带），它吸收掉了300–350纳米的一部分。结果在450–480纳米附近达到峰值——"天空蓝"，即一个宽混合色，而非单色。</p>

      <h3>角分布与偏振结构</h3>
      <p>(1+cos²θ) 相位函数意味着前向和后向散射最强。瑞利散射使散射场在垂直于散射平面的方向上偏振；偏振度在 θ = 90° 时最大（理想纯瑞利极限下为100%），沿太阳方向和反太阳方向为零。白天观测到的偏振度可达约70–80%，因多次散射和气溶胶而降低。天空在反太阳点附近最蓝、最饱和。</p>

      <h3>为什么日落是红色的</h3>
      <p>太阳高度角很低时，斜程大气质量按 ~sec z 增长（接近地平线时发散，大气质量 ≳ 30–40）。直射光束遭受 exp(−τ(λ)) 衰减，且 τ(450 nm)/τ(700 nm) ≈ 5.8，因此直射太阳光谱强烈红移，而头顶的散射天光仍保持蓝色——这就是经典的"红日蓝穹"对比。气溶胶（米氏散射，波长依赖性平坦得多）使红色更加浓重。</p>

      <h3>瑞利图像未涵盖的内容</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>气溶胶（r ≳ λ）的<strong>米氏散射</strong>：前向峰突出，对波长依赖性弱；主导霾和污染天空的白色外观。</li>
        <li><strong>云</strong>：液滴 r ≫ λ → 几何光学/米氏散射区域，各波长几乎被等量散射 → 白色/灰色。</li>
        <li><strong>非弹性通道</strong>：存在转动/振动拉曼散射，但弹性（瑞利）线占绝对主导。</li>
        <li><strong>系外行星类比</strong>：瑞利散射解释了海王星/天王星的蓝色（CH₄ 吸收掉红光），而泰坦（土星卫星）的橙色天空是光化学霾（气溶胶米氏散射），而非分子瑞利散射。</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>历史注记</h3>
      <p>瑞利勋爵（1871年）针对连续介电球体推导了 1/λ⁴ 规律；J. J. 汤姆森独立得到了偶极散射结果。量子（光子）描述将其复现为束缚电子系统的弹性散射，其中偶极矩阵元给出相同的频率依赖关系。</p>
